    ":question/problem": "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=$",
    ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
    ":question/worked-solution": "Here, the first factor is $4\\times 1$ matrix and the second factor is a $1\\times 3$ matrix. \n\nThere is $1$ column in the first matrix and $1$ row in the second matrix, so the product is well defined:\n\n$$\n4\\times \\boxed{\\text{1}}✓\\boxed{\\text{1}}\\times 3=4\\times 3\n$$\n\nComputing the product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}8 & -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & =\\begin{bmatrix}4\\cdot 8 & 4\\cdot (-4) & 4\\cdot 1 \\\\ 0\\cdot 8 & 0\\cdot (-4) & 0\\cdot 1 \\\\ (-1)\\cdot 8 & (-1)\\cdot (-4) & (-1)\\cdot 1 \\\\ 0\\cdot 8 & 0\\cdot (-4) & 0\\cdot 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}32 & -16 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -8 & 4 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
